# Running the server

The server is a single binary, `wyrd-server`. Run it with no subcommand and it serves. Two operator subcommands exist: `init`, which establishes the deployment's platform administrative root, and `recover-root`, which reissues a credential for that root when every platform credential is gone.

## Command line

```bash
# Serve (the default when no subcommand is given).
wyrd-server

# Serve only one transport.
wyrd-server --mode http
wyrd-server --mode grpc

# Establish this deployment's administrative root, then exit.
wyrd-server init

# Reissue a credential for the existing root, then exit.
wyrd-server recover-root
```

`--mode` overrides the `serve.mode` config field (`WYRD_SERVE_MODE`). Accepted values are `both`, `http`, and `grpc`. The default is `both`.

The process uses two non-zero exit codes: `78` for a configuration error (bad env var, invalid config file, failed validation) and `70` for any other fatal error during boot or serving. A clean shutdown exits `0`.

### init

A fresh deployment has no administrative identity at all — not even a tenant. `init` creates the platform administrative root, grants it the platform control plane, issues its first credential, and prints that credential once to stdout. Everything else an operator does, including creating the first tenant, is an ordinary authenticated API call made with it.

```bash
wyrd-server init
# wyrd_global_… (shown once, store it now)
```

The credential is shown exactly once and stored only as an Argon2 verifier. It is printed to your terminal rather than emitted through the server's log pipeline, and server start creates no administrative state and prints no credential under any deployment profile.

Initialization happens at most once. The root's name is unique in the database, so a second `init` — concurrent or later — refuses rather than minting a second root. All three writes commit together, so a failure leaves the deployment uninitialized and safe to retry.

If you lose the credential, issue a replacement against the same principal rather than re-initializing. If you lose *every* platform credential, `wyrd-server recover-root` reissues one for the existing root — see [Recovery](#recovery).

`init` needs the platform-admin database environment set, but not a signing key or storage backend. You can run it against a fresh production database before the full serving environment is in place.

## Administering the deployment

Everything after `init` is an ordinary authenticated call. The `wyrd` CLI
projects the same routes the SDKs call, so no operator task on this page needs
SQL.

The platform plane is reached with the credential `init` printed. Export it
once and every `wyrd platform` command below picks it up:

```bash
export WYRD_SERVER_URL=https://wyrd.example
export WYRD_PLATFORM_CREDENTIAL=wyrd_global_…
```

### Tenants

```bash
# Create a tenant. The administrative credential is printed once.
wyrd platform tenant create --slug acme --display-name "Acme"

# See the directory, in every lifecycle state.
wyrd platform tenant list
wyrd platform tenant inspect --tenant <tenant-id>

# Freeze admission without destroying anything, then restore it.
wyrd platform tenant suspend --tenant <tenant-id>
wyrd platform tenant resume  --tenant <tenant-id>
```

Suspension stops the tokens the tenant has already minted, not only its next
credential exchange, and destroys nothing: rows, grants, and credentials all
survive, so `resume` restores exactly what was there.

### Tenant administration

Inside a tenant, administration uses the tenant's own credential, never the
platform credential. `tenant create` printed that credential once — an API
key. Put it in the environment rather than on the command line; Wyrd exchanges
it for a token itself, so there is no separate exchange step:

```bash
export WYRD_API_KEY=<the admin_credential tenant create printed>

# Configure first: the issuer this tenant's workloads federate from. Wyrd
# resolves the JWKS endpoint from the issuer's own discovery document.
# `wyrd auth` and `wyrd principal revoke` read WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN.
WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN="$WYRD_API_KEY" wyrd auth trusted-issuer add \
  --issuer https://idp.example.com \
  --expected-audience wyrd \
  --client-id wyrd \
  --client-auth Public \
  --claim-subject sub \
  --principal-kind Workload \
  --default-role reader
WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN="$WYRD_API_KEY" wyrd auth trusted-issuer list

# A principal narrower than the tenant administrator.
wyrd principal credential create-principal --name ci-runner --role reader

# Rotate with an overlap: issue, deploy, verify, then retire the old one.
wyrd principal credential issue  --principal <principal-id>
wyrd principal credential list   --principal <principal-id>
wyrd principal credential revoke --principal <principal-id> --credential-id <old-id>

# Compromise, not rotation: suspend the principal so it mints no new tokens.
WYRD_ACCESS_TOKEN="$WYRD_API_KEY" wyrd principal revoke <principal-id> --kind service --reason "key found in a build log"
```

The overlap is the point of issuing before revoking: both credentials are live
between the two commands, so nothing is down while the new one is deployed.
Listing includes revoked credentials on purpose, so an operator can confirm the
superseded one really is gone.

Revoking a *principal* suspends it: none of its credentials can mint another
token, and a user's refresh sessions end. Tokens it already holds are
five-minute snapshots and lapse at expiry, so a compromised identity stops
working within five minutes without any per-request lookup.

### Recovery

Two different losses, two different commands:

| What was lost | Recover with | Needs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A tenant's last administrative credential | `wyrd platform tenant recover-admin --tenant <id>` | The platform credential |
| One of several platform credentials | `wyrd platform credential issue --principal <platform-principal-id>` | Another live platform credential |
| Every platform credential | `wyrd-server recover-root` | This machine's platform-admin database access |

Tenant recovery issues a replacement credential for the tenant's *existing*
administrative principal, so the tenant's roles, resources, and history are
untouched. It refuses a tenant that is not active: a suspended, failed, or
still-provisioning tenant must not acquire a new durable secret.

Rotate the platform credential with an overlap — issue the replacement, verify
it, then revoke the old one — so the deployment is never down between the two
steps:

```bash
wyrd platform credential issue  --principal <platform-principal-id>
wyrd platform credential list   --principal <platform-principal-id>
wyrd platform credential revoke --principal <platform-principal-id> --credential-id <old-id>
```

If that overlap is lost and no platform credential remains, the API cannot help:
every way to obtain a platform session needs a credential, and only a verifier is
stored, so nothing can be read back. `wyrd-server recover-root` is the way in. It
runs from a machine with the deployment's platform-admin database access — the
same authority that ran `init` — and reissues a credential for the root the
deployment already has:

```bash
wyrd-server recover-root
# wyrd_global_… (shown once, store it now)
```

It creates no identity and writes no grant, so the root's authority and audit
history are unchanged, and it leaves the root's other credentials alone — retire
those through the platform plane if they are genuinely lost. It refuses on an
uninitialized deployment; run `init` there instead. Because it needs only
database access, treat that access as equivalent to holding the platform
credential.

<Aside title="Custody">

In a hosted deployment the platform credential is the provider's, not the
tenant's: it administers every tenant on the deployment and grants nothing
inside any of them. Tenants receive only their own tenant credential. Keep the
platform credential in the same custody as the signing key — a secret manager,
not a shell history — and rotate it on the same schedule.

</Aside>

## Serve modes

The server speaks two protocols. HTTP serves the REST/JSON API that your clients, the SDK, the CLI, and the UI call: card registration, queries, admin. gRPC is the ingest transport that machine workers use. They are independent, which is the reason `--mode` exists.

| Mode | HTTP | gRPC | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `both` | yes | yes | The default. Full server. |
| `http` | yes | no | REST/JSON only, no ingest transport. |
| `grpc` | no | yes | Ingest-only workers. |

Most deployments run `both`. Split them when you want the two transports to scale or deploy separately: an API fleet on `http` and an ingest fleet on `grpc`. Running `http` alone also keeps the ingest path off a public listener. gRPC assembly needs a token verifier, so in `http` mode the gRPC router is never built and an HTTP-only deployment does not require it.

## Boot sequence

Boot is ordered and fails closed. A failure at any step stops the process with a non-zero exit and a logged reason. Nothing half-starts.

<WyrdFlowDiagram
  title="Boot sequence"
  steps={[
    { label: 'Load config', sub: 'env > TOML > defaults, then validate' },
    { label: 'Init telemetry', sub: 'tracer provider, log filter' },
    { label: 'Boot Postgres', sub: 'resolve DSNs, run migrations' },
    { label: 'Assemble state', sub: 'pools, storage, OLAP catalog' },
    { label: 'Install auth', sub: 'signing key, verifier, resolvers' },
    { label: 'Seed federation', sub: 'trusted issuers, workload bindings' },
    { label: 'Validate', sub: 'production_validate, fail closed' },
    { label: 'Bind and supervise', sub: 'listeners up, tasks spawned' }
  ]}
/>

Two steps are the most common boot failures:

- Install auth fails closed in a production profile with no signing key. Set `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE` (or `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_PEM`). In development the server mints an ephemeral key and logs a warning, so local runs work with no secret.
- Seed federation fails if a never-seeded trusted issuer is unreachable, or if `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG` names a tenant that does not exist. On a restart, an already-seeded issuer that is temporarily unreachable is a warning, not a boot failure.

See [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/) for every field these steps read.

## Graceful shutdown

Send `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM`. The server stops accepting new connections, lets in-flight requests finish within `shutdown.drain_ms` (default 15s), flushes telemetry, and exits `0`. Requests still running when the drain deadline passes are aborted.

In Kubernetes, set `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` above `shutdown.drain_ms` so the drain completes before the pod is killed.

## Health and readiness

Two unauthenticated endpoints back liveness and readiness probes:

| Endpoint | Answers | Backed by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `GET /healthz` | Is the process alive? | Always `200 ok` once serving. |
| `GET /readyz` | Can it serve traffic? | Cached Postgres and storage probe results. |

`/readyz` reports a snapshot refreshed every `readiness.tick_ms` (default 5s) by a background worker, so a probe never blocks on a live database query. A failing dependency flips the snapshot to not-ready with a reason. The same snapshot drives the gRPC health service.

## Metrics

When `metrics.enabled` is true (the default), the server serves Prometheus metrics on a dedicated listener. By default that listener binds loopback (`127.0.0.1`) on the HTTP port plus one, so `/metrics` is not exposed publicly unless you set `WYRD_METRICS_BIND` yourself. It records `wyrd_http_requests_total` and `wyrd_http_request_duration_seconds` per route, method, and status.

